  "lessons": [
    {
      "id": "ES-C344-cero",
      "sequence": 6120,
      "title": "cero — zero, the empty one, and the word English borrowed twice",
      "headword": "cero",
      "romanization": "cero",
      "gloss": "zero — Arabic sifr, empty; English took the same word twice, as zero and as cipher",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:08513cacf0d52b31",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say the age, the era, then the century. (La época; el siglo.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know first",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "uno, dos, tres, cuatro, cinco — the counting this completes."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "SE-ro, two beats with the weight on the first. The c before e is soft, an s across the Americas. see pronunciation reference."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "cero — \"zero.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Roman numerals have no zero. You cannot write it: there is no symbol, because there was no idea. Counting started at one, and nothing was not a number."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The zero arrived with Arabic mathematics, and it brought its name. Arabic ṣifr meant empty — a translation of the Sanskrit śūnya, also empty. Latin took it as zephirum and cifra, and the two forms went separate ways across Europe."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "English ended up borrowing the same Arabic word twice. From zephirum through Italian came zero. From cifra came cipher — which in older English means a nought, and also a secret code, because Arabic numerals looked like unreadable marks to people used to Roman ones. A cipher can be a nobody, a zero, or a code, and all three are the same borrowed emptiness."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Spanish kept both as well: cero is the number, and la cifra is a digit or a figure."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Counting has a floor under it now."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "cero",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *cero*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "cero o uno",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *cero o uno*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "el número cero",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *el número cero*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 6,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What did Arabic ṣifr mean? (Empty.) And which two English words came from it? (Zero and cipher.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ES-C344-numero",
      "sequence": 6130,
      "title": "el número — the number, and the letter English added by mistake",
      "headword": "el número",
      "romanization": "el número",
      "gloss": "the number — Latin numerus; English number, numeral, numerous, enumerate and numb all come off it",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:4ba026214419fb15",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say the century, then zero. (El siglo; cero.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know first",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "cero — the one this now counts from."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "uno, dos, tres, cuatro, cinco — what a number names."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "NU-me-ro, the weight on the first beat, which is why it takes a written accent. Three even, short vowels. see pronunciation reference."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "el número — \"the number.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Latin numerus, a number. The straight line from Latin to Spanish, with almost nothing happening on the way."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What is worth noticing is what happened in English instead. Latin numerus went into French as nombre, and English took it as number — with a b that was never in the Latin word. It crept in because m followed by r is awkward to say, and a b slips into the gap without anybody deciding to put it there. English did the same in chamber and slumber."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Spanish never added it. Número keeps the Latin shape exactly, so the Spanish word is closer to the original than the English one is."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The rest of the family is clean. A numeral is the written sign. Numerous means many. To enumerate is to go through one by one. And something innumerable cannot be counted at all."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "A word for the count itself, on top of the counting."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "el número",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *el número*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "el número dos",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *el número dos*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "¿cuál es el número?",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *¿cuál es el número?*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 6,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Where did the b in English number come from? (It slipped in between m and r; the Latin never had it.) And which language kept the Latin shape? (Spanish — número.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ES-C344-mitad",
      "sequence": 6140,
      "title": "la mitad — the half, built from middle with an ending you own",
      "headword": "la mitad",
      "romanization": "la mitad",
      "gloss": "the half — Latin medietatem, the middle-ness, from medius middle; English medium, median, mediate and moiety come off it, and the -dad ending is the one you know",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:0dd68c3a7ca62388",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say zero, then the number. (Cero; el número.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know first",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "-dad — the ending, for the fourth time."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "mediodía, medianoche — the same middle, doing the clock."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "mi-TAD, the weight on the second beat. The final -d is very soft and often fades away entirely. see pronunciation reference."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "la mitad — \"the half.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Two things you already own are inside it."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The ending is -dad, from Latin -tātem — the one you have met in verdad, ciudad and edad, and which English turns into -ty. It makes an abstract noun: the state of being such-and-such."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The stem is medius, middle — the same medius sitting in mediodía and medianoche, the middle of the day and the middle of the night."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Put them together and Latin medietātem means middle-ness: the state of being in the middle. A half is what you get when you cut at the middle, so middle-ness became a half, and Spanish wore medietatem down to mitad."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "English took medius many ways: a medium is in the middle, a median splits a road, to mediate is to stand between. And English has the very same word as mitad, though almost nobody uses it: moiety means a half, and came from medietatem through French."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Numbers can now be broken as well as counted."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "la mitad",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *la mitad*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "la mitad del agua",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *la mitad del agua*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "una mitad y otra mitad",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *una mitad y otra mitad*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 6,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Which two pieces make mitad? (Medius, middle, plus the -tātem that gives -dad.) And what does medietātem mean word for word? (Middle-ness.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ES-C344-docena",
      "sequence": 6150,
      "title": "la docena — the dozen, which is two and ten said quickly",
      "headword": "la docena",
      "romanization": "la docena",
      "gloss": "the dozen — Latin duodecim, two-ten, from duo and decem; English dozen and duodecimal are the same twelve",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:8d067e3cc764e228",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say the number, then the half. (El número; la mitad.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know first",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "uno, dos, tres, cuatro, cinco — where counting started."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "el número — the word for the count."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "do-SE-na, the weight on the middle beat. The c before e is soft, and the d is soft between vowels. see pronunciation reference."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "la docena — \"the dozen,\" a set of twelve."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Latin counted twelve as duodecim — duo two plus decem ten. Two and ten, said as one word, which is exactly how English says thirteen and fourteen but stops doing right before it reaches twelve."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Spanish wore duodecim down to doce, and then added the ending -ena, which makes a group of that size. So docena is a twelve-ful, a set counted out by twelves."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "English took the same Latin word by the French road and got dozen, which is duodecim squashed nearly flat. Kept whole, it is duodecimal — counting in twelves, the system behind twelve inches, twelve months and twenty-four hours."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The duo half is everywhere in English: a duo, a duet, a duel — and double, which is next."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "A group can be counted as one thing."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "la docena",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *la docena*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "una docena",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *una docena*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "media docena",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *media docena*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 6,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What is duodecim, taken apart? (Duo two plus decem ten.) And what does the -ena ending do? (Makes a group of that size.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ES-C344-doble",
      "sequence": 6160,
      "title": "doble — double, which means folded in two",
      "headword": "doble",
      "romanization": "doble",
      "gloss": "double, twice as much — Latin duplus, from duo two and -plus fold; English double, duplicate, duplex and duplicity are the same two folds",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:5ee7ea159cf08bb9",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say the half, then the dozen. (La mitad; la docena.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know first",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "la docena — the last of the counting words."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "la mitad — what this undoes."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "DO-ble, two beats with the weight on the first. The b is soft between vowels, made without quite closing the lips. see pronunciation reference."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "doble — \"double,\" twice as much."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Latin duplus is duo, two, plus -plus, which is a fold. So duplus is twofold — and English double says the same thing by the same route, through French."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "That -plus is a hidden joint in a great many words, and once you see it you cannot stop. Triple is threefold. Multiple is many-fold. Simple is sim-plus, onefold — a thing with only one layer, which is why simple means uncomplicated. English even has the plain Germanic version alongside: twofold, threefold, manifold."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The two-fold has a dark side as well. Duplicity is being folded in two — having a second, hidden layer — which is precisely what we accuse a double-dealer of. A duplex has two folds of floor, and to duplicate is to make the second fold."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Against it stands la mitad, which cuts back to one."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Five counting words: cero, el número, la mitad, la docena, doble. Nothing counted, halved or doubled is out of reach."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "doble",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *doble*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "el doble",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *el doble*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "doble o mitad",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *doble o mitad*]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 6,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What does the -plus in duplus mean? (A fold.) And what is simple, taken apart? (Sim-plus — onefold.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
